WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.960
For nine years, the United States conducted one&nbsp;
of the biggest bombing campaigns in the history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:25.920
of the world, against a country pretty much&nbsp;
no American had any idea they were fighting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.960
The country they were targeting is not one&nbsp;
you’ve heard a lot about: it’s not Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:40.320
or Korea, or Vietnam, or Iraq. It’s a country that&nbsp;
is totally unknown to the vast majority of people.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.160
This is the story of Laos.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:24.160
Laos is located right here, in inland Southeast&nbsp;
Asia – north of Cambodia, and west of Vietnam.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.720
Historically, of all these&nbsp;
countries, Laos was the poorest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.120
the least populated, and the least influential.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.840
Since the end of the nineteenth century, Vietnam,&nbsp;
Cambodia, and Laos had been French possessions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.960
grouped together as “French Indochina.”&nbsp;
But the decades after the Second World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.600
saw a liberation struggle&nbsp;
against European colonialism.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:53.840
“The year is 1954. The once mighty French&nbsp;
colonial empire in Southeast Asia is doomed.”

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.480
So along with Cambodia and Vietnam,&nbsp;
Laos became an independent nation.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.880
But, like its two sister&nbsp;
countries, Laos was split:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.280
between royalists and conservatives on the&nbsp;
one hand, and communist rebels – the “Pathet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.880
Lao” – on the other. So, along with the rest&nbsp;
of Indochina, the country entered a civil war.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.720
On its own, the war would&nbsp;
probably not have been so bad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.080
The first years of the conflict, in&nbsp;
fact, were marked by relative calm.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.000
But when larger and more important Vietnam&nbsp;
descended into civil war in the 1960s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.680
and attracted international involvement, Laos&nbsp;
became a proxy front in a much bigger conflict.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:51.840
The royalists governed a strip of land&nbsp;
in the west, supported by American forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.320
based in Thailand. The Pathet Lao,&nbsp;
which ruled the north and the east,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.760
was propped up by its stronger&nbsp;
ally, communist North Vietnam.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.480
This had less to do with interest in Laos itself&nbsp;
than with a question of military logistics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:19.040
For its supply lines, North Vietnam depended&nbsp;
on a series of roads that the U.S. called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.320
the “Ho Chi Minh trail.” And a big part&nbsp;
of that trail ran through eastern Laos.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:24.831
And destroying the Ho Chi Minh trail was one&nbsp;
of America’s top aims in order to defeat North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.831 --> 00:03:29.680
Vietnam. As Secretary of State Dean Rusk&nbsp;
said, “Laos was only the wart on the hog.”

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.360
So the U.S. planned a military campaign to&nbsp;
destroy the Ho Chi Minh trail and defeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:39.840
the Pathet Lao who were protecting it.&nbsp;
They called it “Operation Barrel Roll.”

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.440
But there was something important. It would&nbsp;
have to remain top secret. No mention of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.880
was ever to be made to the public, not even&nbsp;
to the United States Senate. It was called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.200
“the secret war,” and it was primarily&nbsp;
operated not by the military, but by the CIA.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.840
On the ground, the U.S. would supply a warlord&nbsp;
army composed of the country’s ethnic minorities.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.360
“The war involved more than&nbsp;
just Laotians: these tribesmen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:15.920
royalists and fiercely anti-communist, were often&nbsp;
called the Secret Army because they were supplied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:21.600
paid and organized entirely by money and officials&nbsp;
from America’s Central Intelligence Agency.”

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.360
The Secret Army was supplied&nbsp;
by a commercial airline called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.800
“Air America” — in reality, a CIA front.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:34.320
The warlords used Air America to smuggle opium&nbsp;
into Vietnam in order to fund their operations.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.200
And from above, American planes&nbsp;
would attack at all hours of the day.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.680
“Washington never admitted the&nbsp;
scale of the bombing, nor admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:50.320
film coverage of it. The only footage released&nbsp;
showed rice distribution to the refugees.”

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.840
The scale of the bombing was enormous.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.560
Just look at this graphic created by Mother Jones.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:11.120
From 1964 to 1973, the United States dropped more&nbsp;
than two million tons of bombs on Laos. The U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.400
dropped more bombs just on Laos than it dropped&nbsp;
during the entirety of the Second World War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:23.520
on both Germany and Japan. A bomb was dropped an&nbsp;
average of every eight minutes, twenty four hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:31.760
a day, for nine years. The country would&nbsp;
be bombed as much as 300 times a day.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.600
The Plain of Jars in northern Laos&nbsp;
– so called because of its ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:48.320
megalithic rock structures – became the&nbsp;
most intensely bombed place on earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:57.200
In 1960, the Plain of Jars had a population of a&nbsp;
hundred and fifty thousand people; by 1970, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:05.840
9,000 were left. Of the 83,000 water buffalo in&nbsp;
the province, 50,000 were killed by the bombings.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.440
To the peasants and tribespeople of Laos,&nbsp;
the bombing campaign was like something out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:17.760
of a nightmare. One villager said: “The planes&nbsp;
came like birds and the bombs fell like rain.”

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:24.880
Soon northern and eastern Laos looked like the&nbsp;
surface of the moon, totally barren, pitch black.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:31.520
One U.S. Air Force officer said: “There just&nbsp;
aren’t any villages in northern Laos any more.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.120
Of the roughly ten thousand villages in&nbsp;
the country, thirty five hundred were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:42.320
held by the Pathet Lao; of these, the&nbsp;
majority were obliterated by bombing.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.320
It was not until the early 1970s that&nbsp;
Americans learned just how extensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:11.600
the U.S. bombing of Laos had been. The CIA&nbsp;
had kept it such a close secret that even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:15.840
leading senators were shocked to find out&nbsp;
that America had been bombing for years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.560
In the meantime, an entire nation&nbsp;
had been bombed to the dust.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:27.760
But the surprising thing was that nobody&nbsp;
in America really cared. The bombing was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:33.200
not what got Richard Nixon in trouble. Nobody&nbsp;
in the military or the CIA lost their jobs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:35.680
Laos was an afterthought.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:43.840
And in 1975, after the bombing ended, the&nbsp;
Pathet Lao managed to sweep to victory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.200
causing their rivals to flee&nbsp;
to Thailand and America.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.320
But the legacy of the bombing remained&nbsp;
powerful. Because 30 percent of the bombs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.680
had never gone off. So the landscape of Laos&nbsp;
was still covered with unexploded munitions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.760
which would detonate and kill people when touched.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.560
In the decades since the bombing ended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.760
thousands of Laotians have been&nbsp;
permanently disfigured by bombs.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:21.200
There were so many bombs lying around that&nbsp;
some restaurants used them as decorations.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:26.320
Schools put up warnings so that children&nbsp;
would know to avoid unexploded bombs.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.240
Kids even earned money by dismantling bomb scraps.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.360
Since the 1970s, about twenty thousand&nbsp;
Laotians have been killed by unexploded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:40.720
bombs, often while farming.&nbsp;
A massacre in slow motion.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.680
About a quarter of villages in Laos&nbsp;
still have unexploded munitions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:48.720
40 percent of those killed were children.

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:53.840
Laos was once called the&nbsp;
“Land of a Million Elephants.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.360
Now it’s the Land of a Million Unexploded Bombs.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:13.040
In 2016, the U.S. government finally&nbsp;
admitted what it had done in Laos.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.440
Still, what America did in Laos remains obscure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.840
It’s not taught in schools in&nbsp;
America. It is totally forgotten.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.960
And the destruction in Laos&nbsp;
didn’t teach the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:46.080
government any lessons about how to&nbsp;
treat other countries in the future.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:50.720
“Our two governments will&nbsp;
continue to have differences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:55.600
That’s true with many nations. As we do&nbsp;
around the world, the US will continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:00.320
speak up on behalf of what we consider&nbsp;
universal human rights, including the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:04.720
rights of the people of Laos to express&nbsp;
yourselves freely and decide your own future.”

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:13.200
In the 1960s and ‘70s, Laotians had wanted to&nbsp;
determine their own future, among themselves—to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:18.960
determine what type of country they would be.&nbsp;
American bombing was designed to stop that.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:26.800
And that pattern of force never really stopped.&nbsp;
In the decades after, the American strategy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:35.200
overwhelming air power would be used all over&nbsp;
the world. A strategy of “shock and awe.”

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:43.600
And each time, the worst consequences would&nbsp;
be borne not by the fighters on any side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:59.840
but by innocent people who did&nbsp;
nothing to deserve what came to them.

